  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is an inequality: \(\frac{7x+6}2\leq3x+2\)
  </item>
</list>
<div>Select <emphasis>two</emphasis> of the values that are a solution to the inequality.
  </div>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent">
    <item>\( x = -3 \)</item>
    <item>\( x = -2 \)</item>
    <item>\( x = -1 \)</item>
    <item>\( x = 0 \)</item>
    <item>\( x = 1 \)</item>
    <item>\( x = 2 \)</item>
    <item>\( x = 3 \)</item>
  </list>

<div><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\)</div><para>\(\frac{7x+6}{2} \le 3x+2\)</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>Which number line is represented by the inequality \( x ≤ 6 \)?<list>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9c5dde2d476cfd6b22bf6f0d8b5acb0862ed7e79"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0b925536b0ea0b7f58d7e36b38257ff2ac39aa91"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8cbb7032815b55597bfb00dd315a9d9f2a6719a9"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6bfd8560461129262b0ae3ad730b0a1c85a9d8a3"/></media></figure></item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8cbb7032815b55597bfb00dd315a9d9f2a6719a9"/></media></figure></para>
<para>\(x \le 6\) means that the solution would be any number less than or equal to 6.</para>

<para>For questions 3 – 4, use the following information:</para>
<blockquote><para>Scooter rental company A charges $9 per hour plus a $11 initiation fee. Scooter rental company B charges $15 per hour.</para></blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>A customer writes the following inequality to represent the situation: \(9h + 11 &gt; 15h\).</item>
</list>
<para>Which of the following is true about the given inequality?</para>
  <list>
    <item>Company A costs more than Company B.</item>
    <item>Company A costs less than Company B.</item>
    <item>Company A costs the same as Company B.</item>
    <item>\( h \) represents the number of scooters rented.</item>
  </list>

  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>: Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
  <para>Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>For which number of hours does Company B cost less?
    <list>
      <item>1 hour</item>
      <item>2 hours</item>
      <item>3 hours</item>
      <item>4 hours</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer :</emphasis> 1 hour</para>
<para>
  Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.</para>
<para>
  Substitute the number 2 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 2 hours.</para>
<para>
    Substitute the number 3 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 3 hours.</para>
<para>
  Substitute the number 4 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 4 hours.</para>
<para>Company B costs the least for 1 hour.</para>


<para>For questions 5 – 7, use the following information:</para><blockquote><para>Cycle Rentals offers 20 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike. Bikes Unlimited offers 5 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike plus 60 points for joining the membership club.</para></blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>The equation \( 20x = 5x + 60 \) represents a possible constraint about the situation.<u>?</u>__
  </item>
</list>
<div>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The correct answer is 4.</para>
<para>
  \(20x=5x+60\)</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint you found in the previous question?</item>
  <list>
    <item>Cycle Rentals</item>
    <item>Bikes Unlimited</item>
    </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Cycle Rentals</para>
<para>
  The constraint from question 5 is \(x=4\). </para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which number line represents the solution to the inequality, \( 20x &gt; 5x + 60 \), representing the situation when you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals?
    <list>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8006903b8bee6446e2bf991eedd28c997ef3d0f4"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2cdbc8eae1d8ce8f1f626cbccc9d0285c406b8a1"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d320204ee9b51df65b814e8ddcbb82312b7809ad"/></media></figure></item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> <figure><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9baf590bad3d5c61a1f40792b71b0ba0e6e5f71b"/></media></figure></para>
<para>
  Solve for \(x\).</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which number line represents the solution to \( 5+8x&lt;3(2x+4) \)?
    <list>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from -10 to 10 with an open circle on the point –3.5. The number line to the right of –3.5 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/24b00cad1e514adf5b68db96506d38fb25aaf677"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from -10 to 10 with an open circle on the point –3.5. The number line to the left of –3.5 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5e52f4f9c15fc02f77cc8e962ff6eb35698a975f"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from -10 to 10 with an open circle on the point 3.5. The number line to the right of 3.5 is shaded.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a22f6aa3de031d8691fef966087b580e8c77d21c"/></media></figure></item>
      <item><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from -10 to 10 with an open circle on the point 3.5. The number line to the left of 3.5 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a3dfa5c4eb28bc1ab6a159329d0ad49e1847a20e"/></media></figure></item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> D <figure><media alt="A number line is shown from -10 to 10 with an open circle on the point 3.5. The number line to the left of 3.5 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a3dfa5c4eb28bc1ab6a159329d0ad49e1847a20e"/></media></figure></para><para>
  Solve for \(x\).</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the solution set to this inequality by solving the related equation: \( 2x-3 &gt; \frac{2x-5}{2} \).
    <list>
      <item>\( x &lt; \frac12\)</item>
      <item>\( x &gt; \frac{1}{2} \)</item>
      <item>\( x \leq \frac{1}{2} \)</item>
      <item>\( x \geq\frac{1}{2} \)</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x &gt; \frac12\)</para>
<para>
  Solve for \(x\).</para>



<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is an inequality: \( \frac{-10+x}{4} + 5 \geq  \frac{7x-5}{3} \)</item>
</list>
<para>What value of \(x\) will produce equality (or make the two sides equal)?</para><para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>  2</para>
<para>
  Solve for \(x\).</para>
<!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
<para>
  \(\begin{align*}
  -30+3x+60&amp;=28x-20 \\
  3x+30&amp;=28x-20\\
  3x+50&amp;=28x\\
  50&amp;=25x\\
  2&amp;=x\\
  \end{align*}\)</para>
<!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
<para>
  \(\frac{-10+2}{4}+5\)</para>
<para>
  \(\frac{(7)(2)-5}{3}\)</para>
</div></section>
